 :question/id #uuid "14bb2d65-c2fa-5181-9d50-ff6fa898ca5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-45602" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeafe81f-2d40-5413-8d19-881972161400" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Output of an Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89c513db-b3c1-5e59-ac22-9135ece7cd3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186114146} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c1aaf4-12ab-546f-9195-b9891d12fb64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186114147} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ec7b89-74ed-5ef1-b81b-13c08e162ac0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id 17592186114148} {:answer/id #uuid "ed919af0-ab3a-59de-bb77-9c9a1a9f88d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id 17592186114149} {:answer/id #uuid "2adb52f2-975c-579c-b31f-59703a1e5a79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id 17592186114150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed919af0-ab3a-59de-bb77-9c9a1a9f88d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id 17592186114149} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1f24b2-6672-5293-99ef-07e0e6f5b77d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186114145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "The mass, in metric tons, of a meteor is modeled by the function $m(t)=20\\cdot (0.4)^{t},$ where $t$ represents the time, in minutes, since the meteor entered the Earth's atmosphere. In metric tons, how much mass did the meteor lose between the second and third minutes after entering the Earth's atmosphere?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to find the total mass that the meteor lost between $t=2$ and $t=3$ minutes. To calculate this, we find the meteor's mass after $2$ minutes, and we subtract its mass after $3$ minutes. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3).\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=2$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(2) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.16 \\\\  & =3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=3$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(3) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{3} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.064 \\\\  & =1.28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass lost between the second and third minute is\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3)=3.2-1.28=1.92.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the meteor's mass decreased by $1.92$ metric tons between the second and third minutes."} {:db/id 17592186115869 :question/id #uuid "977226b8-4490-5f1a-950f-1ae37c9b67f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-45730"} {:db/id 17592186508972 :question/id #uuid "bce8526b-2382-5fd4-882b-274b4364bd61" :question/math-academy-id "q-112525"} {:db/id 17592186508980 :question/id #uuid "d33dc350-97af-5862-9547-76e026eaf801" :question/math-academy-id "q-64120"} {:db/id 17592186557278 :question/id #uuid "8c286c29-c804-5f9e-952f-d64fbcc60ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-45713" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeafe81f-2d40-5413-8d19-881972161400" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Output of an Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "120cc5f5-b994-5fe7-879c-93dded23b4f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,900" :db/id 17592186556465} {:answer/id #uuid "62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,875" :db/id 17592186556656} {:answer/id #uuid "8af9a23c-445a-5275-9661-7299cac973d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,875" :db/id 17592186556762} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee33d95-d119-584c-b8ee-7dac64f9c29c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\,000" :db/id 17592186556817} {:answer/id #uuid "fb544b2f-30e9-5368-a619-27b767c38ae4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,675" :db/id 17592186557052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,875" :db/id 17592186556656} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cb964d-8a8c-59d6-bbd2-2c379e559c33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "The population $C(t)$ of condors in the Andes is given by $C(t)=1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time, in decades, since the last census. How many condors vanished between the second and third decades since the last census?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the difference in the population of condors between $t=2$ and $t=3$ decades. To calculate this, we find the population after $2$ decades, and we subtract the population after $3$ decades. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=2$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.062\\,5 \\\\  & =62\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=3$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =15\\,625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the number of condors lost between the second and third decades is\n\n$$\nC(3)-C(2)=62\\,500-15\\,625=46\\,875.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $46\\,875$ condors vanished between the second and third decades."} {:db/id 17592186115384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186114949 :question/id #uuid "d550f36c-a6f0-5b37-a998-4d44fa5f1d3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2078"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0f5949-6a1a-5187-a246-8f9bac2e8d58" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186115865 :question/id #uuid "6b0edb44-4f65-5f8c-b3b9-8d9238dbd7ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-45418"} {:db/id 17592186115866 :question/id #uuid "3b529d12-30a0-50ea-a8c0-3efc03b69cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-45455"} {:db/id 17592186508947 :question/id #uuid "836a9482-113e-597c-80f8-a8ed0a6b01c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-85526"} {:db/id 17592186508951 :question/id #uuid "8a688991-2eb3-5f8d-836d-4a680ceee3ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-85527"}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting a Decay Rate"} {:db/id 17592186114949 :question/id #uuid "d550f36c-a6f0-5b37-a998-4d44fa5f1d3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2078"} {:db/id 17592186115865 :question/id #uuid "6b0edb44-4f65-5f8c-b3b9-8d9238dbd7ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-45418"} {:db/id 17592186115866 :question/id #uuid "3b529d12-30a0-50ea-a8c0-3efc03b69cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-45455" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0f5949-6a1a-5187-a246-8f9bac2e8d58" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting a Decay Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9131f976-07ea-52cc-9cba-99a846e52f02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The car increases its value by $85%$ each year" :db/id 17592186114134} {:answer/id #uuid "fbde3adb-07da-5f3e-9471-c7c404c031e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The car increases its value by $15%$ each year" :db/id 17592186114135} {:answer/id #uuid "15751958-c03e-595e-bf69-e4c3ce7d40fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year" :db/id 17592186114136} {:answer/id #uuid "16c7b050-fa51-5bed-8e60-38696acb397a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The value of the car depreciates by $85%$ each year" :db/id 17592186114137} {:answer/id #uuid "78fd8c92-25de-5ac5-bef6-5099c54e2d5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The initial price of the car is $\\$25\\, 500$" :db/id 17592186556707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15751958-c03e-595e-bf69-e4c3ce7d40fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year" :db/id 17592186114136} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32a33fa-fb7f-52f5-a0c8-9f50caf27e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186114133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "The market value of a sports car, in dollars, can be modeled according to the rule $30\\,000\\cdot (0.85)^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the car was manufactured. What does the base of the exponential expression $(0.85)$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=30\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n\n- $1-r=0.85,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observations, in years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.85\\,⟹\\,r=0.15.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.15$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $15%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.85$ means that the value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year."} {:db/id 17592186508947 :question/id #uuid "836a9482-113e-597c-80f8-a8ed0a6b01c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-85526"} {:db/id 17592186508951 :question/id #uuid "8a688991-2eb3-5f8d-836d-4a680ceee3ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-85527"} {:db/id 17592186115385 :know