47 17592186045417] [17592186115384 1038 17592186508951 17592186045417] [17592186115384 1039 "Interpreting a Decay Rate" 17592186045417] [17592186115384 1041 17592186114949 17592186045417] [17592186115384 1042 #uuid "cd0f5949-6a1a-5187-a246-8f9bac2e8d58" 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1037 17592186049657 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1038 17592186115867 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1038 17592186115910 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1038 17592186508950 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1038 17592186508967 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1039 "Interpreting Exponential Expressions" 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1041 17592186114950 17592186045417] [17592186115385 1042 #uuid "ab694f0f-947f-5bba-ad7a-988ea28a97fd" 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1043 17592186116356 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1043 17592186116357 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1043 17592186116358 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1043 17592186116359 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1044 17592186045463 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1045 "Interpreting Exponential Decay" 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1046 17592186116356 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1049 17592186049732 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1050 420.0 17592186045417] [17592186115438 1053 #uuid "c1204165-46fa-568c-a8e1-e13727d2be23" 17592186045417] [17592186115866 1068 17592186114133 17592186045417] [17592186115866 1069 "q-45455" 17592186045417] [17592186115866 1070 17592186045486 17592186045417] [17592186115866 1071 "The market value of a sports car, in dollars, can be modeled according to the rule $30\\,000\\cdot (0.85)^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the car was manufactured. What does the base of the exponential expression $(0.85)$ mean in this context?" 17592186045417] [17592186115866 1072 #uuid "3b529d12-30a0-50ea-a8c0-3efc03b69cc9" 17592186045417] [17592186115866 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=30\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n\n- $1-r=0.85,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observations, in years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.85\\,⟹\\,r=0.15.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.15$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $15%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.85$ means that the value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year." 17592186045417] [17592186115868 1068 17592186114145 17592186045417] [17592186115868 1069 "q-45602" 17592186045417] [17592186115868 1070 17592186045487 17592186045417] [17592186115868 1071 "The mass, in metric tons, of a meteor is modeled by the function $m(t)=20\\cdot (0.4)^{t},$ where $t$ represents the time, in minutes, since the meteor entered the Earth's atmosphere. In metric tons, how much mass did the meteor lose between the second and third minutes after entering the Earth's atmosphere?" 17592186045417] [17592186115868 1072 #uuid "14bb2d65-c2fa-5181-9d50-ff6fa898ca5b" 17592186045417] [17592186115868 1073 true 17592186045417] [17592186115868 1074 "We need to find the total mass that the meteor lost between $t=2$ and $t=3$ minutes. To calculate this, we find the meteor's mass after $2$ minutes, and we subtract its mass after $3$ minutes. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3).\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=2$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(2) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.16 \\\\  & =3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=3$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(3) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{3} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.064 \\\\  & =1.28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass lost between the second and third minute is\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3)=3.2-1.28=1.92.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the meteor's mass decreased by $1.92$ metric tons between the second and third minutes." 17592186045417] [17592186116356 1058 17592186116359 17592186045417] [17592186116356 1061 7129 17592186045417] [17592186116356 1062 #uuid "b36f7829-7634-567d-9517-b3c22769bd8e" 17592186045417] [17592186116356 1063 17592186116434 17592186045417] [17592186116357 1058 17592186116358 17592186045417] [17592186116357 1061 7131 17592186045417] [17592186116357 1062 #uuid "a9eb3a9e-cbd0-5d23-976c-d6e30621bd62" 17592186045417] [17592186116357 1063 17592186115383 17592186045417] [17592186116358 1061 7133 17592186045417] [17592186116358 1062 #uuid "370e94ab-be87-5775-983a-458131b0b569" 17592186045417] [17592186116358 1063 17592186115384 17592186045417] [17592186116359 1058 17592186116357 17592186045417] [17592186116359 1061 7137 17592186045417] [17592186116359 1062 #uuid "07158a3f-d957-5396-9819-37e29b775de6" 17592186045417] [17592186116359 1063 17592186115385 17592186045417] [17592186116434 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to interpret various aspects of an exponential decay expression or function.\n\nFor example, suppose that, due to overfishing, a fish population decays exponentially. The population $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = 5\\,000 \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began. What can we deduce about the population of fish from this model?\n\nThe first thing to notice is that the function above is an exponential decay function of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t.\n$$\n\nSo, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ represents the initial value. So this tells us that there were $5\\,000$ fish when the overfishing began.\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor. Since $0 < b < 1,$ this tells us that we're dealing with exponential decay, *not* exponential growth (for exponential growth we need $b > 1$).\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began." 17592186045417] [17592186116434 1065 1970 17592186045417] [17592186116434 1066 #uuid "adec391e-8573-55bf-b53b-d5d15177f633" 17592186045417] [17592186116434 1067 "Introduction" 17592186045417] [17592186556465 1080 "46\\,900" 17592186045417] [17592186556465 1082 17592186045498 17592186045417] [17592186556465 1083 #uuid "120cc5f5-b994-5fe7-879c-93dded23b4f7" 17592186045417] [17592186556656 1080 "46\\,875" 17592186045417] [17592186556656 1082 17592186045498 17592186045417] [17592186556656 1083 #uuid "62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" 17592186045417] [17592186556707 1080 "The initial price of the car is $\\$25\\, 500$" 17592186045417] [17592186556707 1082 17592186045499 17592186045417] [17592186556707 1083 #uuid "78fd8c92-25de-5ac5-bef6-5099c54e2d5c" 17592186045417] [17592186556762 1080 "40\\,875" 17592186045417] [17592186556762 1082 17592186045498 17592186045417] [17592186556762 1083 #uuid "8af9a23c-445a-5275-9661-7299cac973d5" 17592186045417] [17592186556817 1080 "50\\,000" 17592186045417] [17592186556817 1082 17592186045498 17592186045417] [17592186556817 1083 #uuid "9ee33d95-d119-584c-b8ee-7dac64f9c29c" 17592186045417] [17592186557052 1080 "48\\,675" 17592186045417] [17592186557052 1082 17592186045498 17592186045417] [17592186557052 1083 #uuid "fb544b2f-30e9-5368-a619-27b767c38ae4" 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1075 17592186556465 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1075 17592186556656 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1075 17592186556762 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1075 17592186556817 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1075 17592186557052 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1076 17592186045493 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1077 "selection" 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1078 17592186556656 17592186045417] [17592186557169 1079 #uuid "b9cb964d-8a8c-59d6-bbd2-2c379e559c33" 17592186045417] [17592186557278 1068 17592186557169 17592186045417] [17592186557278 1069 "q-45713" 17592186045417] [17592186557278 1070 17592186045487 17592186045417] [17592186557278 1071 "The population $C(t)$ of condors in the Andes is given by $C(t)=1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time, in decades, since the last census. How many condors vanished between the second and third decades since the last census?" 17592186045417] [17592186557278 1072 #uuid "8c286c29-c804-5f9e-952f-d64fbcc60ae3" 17592186045417] [17592186557278 1074 "We need to find the difference in the population of condors between $t=2$ and $t=3$ decades. To calculate this, we find the population after $2$ decades, and we subtract the population after $3$ decades. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=2$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.062\\,5 \\\\  & =62\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=3$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =15\\,625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the number of condors lost between the second and third decades is\n\n$$\nC(3)-C(2)=62\\,500-15\\,625=46\\,875.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $46\\,875$ condors vanished between the second and third decades." 17592186045417]]}